    specifies no change in training, yet it treats the poor season as if it were merely
    an extreme fluctuation around a stable true ability. That assumption is doing all
    the work, and it is not warranted. An unusually poor season can reflect a real
    decline — injury, illness, motivation, technique breakdown, age-related loss, or
    external disruption — and an average season afterward can simply be partial recovery
    or stabilization at a lower level. Regression to the mean predicts improvement after
